+### ::a26b7a1 An example
+
+::a26b7a2 Consider a debate with three parties.
+
+- ::a26b7a3 Party `a` opens it with the initial contribution `a`. ::a26b7a4 Its segments are keyed `a1`, `a2` and so on.
+- ::a26b7a5 Party `b` disagrees with two points. ::a26b7a6 They answer segment 7 with a contribution keyed `a7b`, and segment 10 with another one keyed `a10b`. ::a26b7a7 The segments inside those contributions are keyed `a7b1`, `a7b2` and `a10b1`, `a10b2`. ::a26b7a8 Note that a contribution key ends with a letter, a segment key with a number.
+- ::a26b7a9 Party `c` joins, reads the exchange and answers segment 4 of `a7b`. ::a26b7a10 Their contribution is `a7b4c`.
+- ::a26b7a11 Party `a` returns to clear up a misunderstanding in the second segment of that answer, which produces `a7b4c2a`.
+
+::a26b7a12 A key is a path. ::a26b7a13 Read from left to right it names every step from the opening contribution down to the answer in front of you, which is why no answer can be detached from what it responds to.
+
+### ::a26b7a14 Why this is worth the trouble
+
+::a26b7a15 A discussion becomes granular. ::a26b7a16 A single point can be addressed precisely, and a disagreement can be followed down to the level where it actually starts — often an assumption that neither side had stated. ::a26b7a17 Meanwhile the wider context is never lost, because every contribution stays anchored where it belongs. ::a26b7a18 And since every segment has a key, referring to something precisely costs nothing.
